WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Elena's employer wants to establish a qualified retirement plan.

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.020
Qualified does not mean the employer bought a particular insurance product

3
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.500
or promised the highest investment return.

4
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.260
The plan must satisfy applicable tax-law requirements in both its written

5
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.080
terms and actual operation.

6
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.820
Its assets must serve the exclusive benefit of participating employees and

7
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.660
their beneficiaries, rather than being diverted for the employer's unrelated

8
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.020
use. Coverage, contributions, benefits, vesting and distribution

9
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.000
provisions must satisfy the relevant rules.

10
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.560
An owner cannot simply label a personal investment a qualified plan

11
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.440
and claim the same treatment.

12
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.380
Start with the legal arrangement and the people it must cover,

13
00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.660
then identify the investments used inside it.

14
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.740
A qualified plan generally must satisfy rules against discrimination in

15
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.760
favor of highly compensated employees.

16
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.180
This is different from requiring every worker to receive exactly the

17
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.140
same dollar amount. A permitted formula can use compensation and other

18
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.540
allowed factors, and specific plan designs have different testing or

19
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.040
safe-harbor requirements. The plan must also follow applicable eligibility

20
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.220
and participation rules. An employer should not assume that part-time

21
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.900
workers can always be ignored.

22
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.280
Compare the actual workforce with the plan's current legal requirements.

23
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.380
On the exam, a proposal to benefit only a favored owner

24
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.220
while improperly excluding eligible employees raises a qualification problem,

25
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.220
even if the employer calls the arrangement a retirement benefit.

26
00:01:37.832 --> 00:01:41.412
The employee has five thousand dollars attributable to her own salary

27
00:01:41.412 --> 00:01:45.252
deferrals and four thousand dollars attributable to employer contributions.

28
00:01:46.112 --> 00:01:47.632
Her own deferrals are fully vested.

29
00:01:48.032 --> 00:01:51.392
Assume the plan's lawful schedule makes sixty percent of that employer

30
00:01:51.392 --> 00:01:54.252
portion vested today, with no other adjustments.

31
00:01:54.572 --> 00:01:57.238
She owns all five thousand dollars of her deferrals plus twenty-four

32
00:01:57.238 --> 00:02:01.152
hundred dollars of the employer portion, for seventy-four hundred dollars of

33
00:02:01.152 --> 00:02:05.632
vested value. Vesting schedules cannot take away her own salary deferrals.

34
00:02:05.812 --> 00:02:09.692
Also separate ownership from access: being fully vested does not necessarily

35
00:02:09.692 --> 00:02:11.792
authorize a withdrawal at any moment.

36
00:02:11.992 --> 00:02:15.832
The plan's distribution rules, tax consequences and any applicable contract

37
00:02:15.832 --> 00:02:17.712
charges still need separate review.

38
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.400
An employer can generally deduct qualifying plan contributions within the

39
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.100
applicable limits. A deduction reduces the income used to calculate tax;

40
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.780
it is not automatically a dollar-for-dollar reduction of the employer's tax

41
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.780
bill. For an employee making an allowed pretax salary deferral,

42
00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.320
that amount generally is excluded from current federal income-tax wages.

43
00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.880
It normally remains subject to applicable Social Security and Medicare

44
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:51.180
payroll taxes. Investment growth generally accumulates without current annual

45
00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.100
income tax inside the qualified arrangement.

46
00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.780
Designated Roth contributions work differently: they are included in current

47
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.760
income, with qualified later distributions potentially excluded.

48
00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.000
Do not teach that every contribution or every eventual benefit is

49
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.320
tax-free merely because the plan is qualified.

50
00:03:11.238 --> 00:03:15.238
Suppose David receives a ten thousand dollar cash distribution from a

51
00:03:15.238 --> 00:03:18.758
qualified plan containing only pretax amounts and untaxed earnings.

52
00:03:19.558 --> 00:03:23.718
No rollover occurs. The full ten thousand dollars generally is ordinary

53
00:03:23.718 --> 00:03:28.978
taxable income. If he is forty-five and no additional-tax exception applies,

54
00:03:29.518 --> 00:03:33.238
another one thousand dollars of federal tax generally results from the

55
00:03:33.238 --> 00:03:35.038
ten percent early-distribution rule.

56
00:03:35.038 --> 00:03:40.138
Reaching age fifty-nine and one half removes that ordinary age-based

57
00:03:40.138 --> 00:03:44.798
additional tax, but does not automatically remove income tax on pretax

58
00:03:44.798 --> 00:03:47.258
money. Some exceptions apply earlier.

59
00:03:48.278 --> 00:03:52.838
For example, a covered employer-plan distribution after separation during or

60
00:03:52.838 --> 00:03:56.198
after the year the worker turns fifty-five can qualify for an

61
00:03:56.198 --> 00:04:00.478
exception. That particular separation exception does not apply to an IRA.

62
00:04:01.362 --> 00:04:05.542
For traditional pretax benefits, required minimum distributions depend on the

63
00:04:05.542 --> 00:04:08.242
applicable age, plan and retirement facts.

64
00:04:08.922 --> 00:04:13.262
Consider an employee reaching age seventy-three in 2026 who still works

65
00:04:13.262 --> 00:04:14.402
for the sponsoring employer.

66
00:04:15.182 --> 00:04:18.102
If the employee is not a five-percent owner and the plan

67
00:04:18.102 --> 00:04:21.682
permits the applicable delay, the required beginning date can wait until

68
00:04:21.682 --> 00:04:25.642
retirement. Do not carry that still-working rule into a traditional IRA

69
00:04:26.342 --> 00:04:30.402
or assume it postpones every former-employer account.

70
00:04:30.942 --> 00:04:34.882
Designated Roth plan accounts no longer have owner lifetime required minimum

71
00:04:34.882 --> 00:04:39.202
distributions under the current rule, although beneficiary requirements

72
00:04:39.202 --> 00:04:44.142
remain. Keep four questions separate: does the plan qualify,

73
00:04:45.142 --> 00:04:49.722
what does the employee own, when may money leave, and how

74
00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:50.842
is that payment taxed?
